---
title: FrontierJS — the schema is the app
description: A schema-seeded fullstack framework. One .lite file seeds your database, your API, and your UI. CRUD, validation, authorization and live updates are derived, not written.
render: static
---
<!--
  The front page, prerendered. Every byte a visitor gets is in the file; there
  is no JavaScript on this page except the theme swatches in the layout, which
  are an island.

  ── Why the code samples are not in this file ──────────────────────────

  Two reasons, and only the second is Mesa's. Mesa reads `{` in markup as the
  start of an expression, so a `<pre>` holding `{#each leads.data as lead}` is
  not a code sample to the compiler — it is a block tag, and the page does not
  compile. That alone would be an argument for hoisting them into a script.

  The one that decides it is that a sample has to be HIGHLIGHTED, and this is
  the page whose README says the code samples are the product. They are source
  in `index.meta.js`, `@frontierjs/toolbelt/glow` marks them up at build time,
  and `@frontierjs/css` themes what it emits — so nothing on this page is a
  code palette, and copying a sample out of the source gives you code rather
  than the HTML the hand-marked version gave you.
-->
<script>
  export let data = null

  // Already HTML by the time it arrives here, which is what {@html} is for.
  const sample = data?.samples ?? {}
</script>

<!-- ─── Hero ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="top">
  <div class="container hero">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">Schema-seeded fullstack framework</p>
      <h1>The schema<br>is the app.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        One <code>.lite</code> file seeds your database, your API, and your UI.
        Write the schema and a six-line service — CRUD, validation, authorization,
        pagination and live updates are <strong>derived, not written</strong>.
      </p>
      <div class="cluster" style="margin-top:1.75rem; gap:.75rem;">
        <a class="btn primary raised" href="#start">Get started</a>
        <a class="btn outlined" href="#code">See the code</a>
        <span class="badge warning" title="Working, but APIs may still move">Alpha</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <pre class="code">{@html sample.SCHEMA}</pre>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── The idea ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="idea">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">The idea</p>
    <h2>One seed, three realms</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:60ch">
      Every framework helps you write the glue between database, server, and browser.
      FrontierJS derives glue you never write — and everything traces back to one file.
    </p>

    <div class="flow" style="margin:1.5rem 0 2rem">
      <span class="hop"><strong>Data</strong> · Model</span>
      <span class="arrow">→</span>
      <span class="hop"><strong>API</strong> · Service</span>
      <span class="arrow">→</span>
      <span class="hop"><strong>UI</strong> · Resource</span>
      <span class="arrow" style="margin-left:.5rem">+</span>
      <span class="hop muted">Release</span>
      <span class="hop muted">Suite</span>
    </div>

    <div class="grid cols3">
      <article class="card stack">
        <h3>Derived, not declared</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Other frameworks give you a config file to describe your routes and queries.
          That is still glue you type. Here the schema already knows the shape, the rules,
          and who may do what — so the API is read out of it, not written beside it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card stack">
        <h3>Authorization travels with the data</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Gates and row policies live on the Model and compile into SQL. A query cannot
          take a code path that forgot to check, because the check is not in the code path —
          it is in the data layer, under every caller.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card stack">
        <h3>One engine on both sides</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          Litestone is SQLite. The same schema, the same query API and the same gates can
          run on the server and in the browser — which is what makes offline-first a
          direction rather than a rewrite.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── See it ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="code">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">See it</p>
    <h2>Schema → Service → Resource</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:60ch">
      The whole application below is the schema in the hero plus these three files.
      Nothing is elided.
    </p>

    <div class="grid cols2" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <div class="stack">
        <h3><code>api/server.ts</code> — the API</h3>
        <pre class="code">{@html sample.API}</pre>
      </div>

      <div class="stack">
        <h3><code>src/routes/leads/index.mesa</code> — the UI</h3>
        <pre class="code">{@html sample.UI}</pre>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <h3>What you did not write</h3>
      <div class="grid cols3" style="margin-top:1rem">
        <ul class="checks">
          <li>Full CRUD at <code>/api/leads</code></li>
          <li><code>400</code>s from the schema's own rules</li>
          <li><code>401</code>/<code>403</code>s from the Model's gate</li>
        </ul>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li>Pagination and a stable result envelope</li>
          <li>Row policies compiled into every query</li>
          <li>TypeScript types for every Model</li>
        </ul>
        <ul class="checks">
          <li>Live WebSocket events on mutation</li>
          <li>Migrations diffed from the schema</li>
          <li>JSON Schema, shared by API and UI</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── Packages ────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="packages">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">The map</p>
    <h2>Twelve packages, one direction</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:60ch">
      <code>Litestone ← Junction ← Sierra</code>, never the reverse.
      Mesa is a leaf with zero workspace dependencies. Use the whole stack, or one piece.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <table class="table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Package</th><th>Realm</th><th>What it is</th><th>State</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>litestone</strong></td><td>Data</td><td><code>.lite</code> language, SQLite client, gates &amp; policies, migrations, tenants, studio</td><td><span class="badge success">v1.1 · 1241 tests</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>junction</strong></td><td>API</td><td>Services, hook pipeline, HTTP/WS transport, channels, browser client</td><td><span class="badge success">703 tests</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>sierra</strong></td><td>UI</td><td>File-tree routing, Vite build, <code>createResource</code>, static prerender</td><td><span class="badge success">558 tests</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>mesa</strong></td><td>UI</td><td>Reactive compiler + signal runtime. Renders to HTML, email, fragment or JS</td><td><span class="badge success">869 tests</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>auth</strong></td><td>Identity</td><td>Native provider over Litestone, ships schema fragments and <code>/auth/*</code></td><td><span class="badge warning">thin coverage</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>caravan</strong></td><td>Jobs</td><td>SQLite job queue and cron, exposed as <code>app.jobs</code></td><td><span class="badge success">67 tests</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>conduit</strong></td><td>Egress</td><td>Outbound boundary — declared targets over http, ws or unix</td><td><span class="badge success">169 tests</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>notifications</strong></td><td>Slice</td><td>In-app record + WebSocket event + email fan-out via <code>app.notify</code></td><td><span class="badge">working</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>cli</strong> (<code>fli</code>)</td><td>Tooling</td><td>Markdown-native command runtime, scaffolds, deploy pipeline</td><td><span class="badge success">142 tests</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>css</strong></td><td>UI</td><td>Semantics-first design system. Plain CSS, no build step — this page uses it</td><td><span class="badge success">v0.10 · 202 tests</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>jetty</strong></td><td>UI</td><td>Browser-extension container — Mesa UI with a service-worker relay</td><td><span class="badge warning">pre-production</span></td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>vscode</strong></td><td>Tooling</td><td>Litestone language server and Mesa editor support</td><td><span class="badge warning">early</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── Extend ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="extend">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Extend</p>
    <h2>Four concepts, everywhere</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:60ch">
      Learn these once and they hold across every realm. That is the whole design
      constraint: keep the mental model small enough to fit in your head.
    </p>

    <div class="grid cols2" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <article class="card stack">
        <h3><span class="pill primary">Declaration</span> change the data's own rules</h3>
        <pre class="code">{@html sample.DECLARATION}</pre>
      </article>

      <article class="card stack">
        <h3><span class="pill primary">Hook</span> step into a pipeline</h3>
        <pre class="code">{@html sample.HOOK}</pre>
      </article>

      <article class="card stack">
        <h3><span class="pill primary">Plugin</span> attach a capability</h3>
        <pre class="code">{@html sample.PLUGIN}</pre>
      </article>

      <article class="card stack">
        <h3><span class="pill primary">Provider</span> swap an implementation</h3>
        <pre class="code">{@html sample.PROVIDER}</pre>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ─── Vision ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
<section id="vision">
  <div class="container stack">
    <p class="eyebrow">Direction</p>
    <h2>Where this is going</h2>
    <p class="lede" style="max-width:60ch">
      Stated plainly as direction, not as shipped features. Everything above this
      section runs today; everything in it does not yet.
    </p>

    <div class="grid cols3" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      <article class="card stack">
        <h3>Slices — installable full-stack modules</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          One package that ships a Model, a Service, a Resource and its own conformance
          tests — install only the parts you want. Take the API without the frontend, and
          have that be checkable rather than hopeful.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card stack">
        <h3>Offline-first, small, self-hosted</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          The same SQLite engine on both sides means the schema, the queries and the gates
          come along. Local enforcement, a sync queue, and an app you can run as one
          binary next to one file.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card stack">
        <h3>One target axis</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted">
          One component set, many outputs: SPA, static, widget, extension — and later
          desktop and mobile. A new platform should be an entry on a list, not a new
          framework to learn.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm text-muted" style="margin-top:1.5rem">
      MIT licensed. No account, no hosted service, no phone-home — self-hosting is the
      default path, not the fallback.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

